This design enforces separation of concerns: data collection and cleaning occur in Python; analytics extract mathematically verified facts; the LLM handles linguistic formatting; and a programmatic trust layer validates, strips ungrounded claims, and scores the report before publication.


% ==================== DETAILED SYSTEM COMPONENT DESIGN ====================
\section{System Component Design and Technical Justification}

\subsection{Ingestion and Storage Layer}
The Ingestion Layer is designed for unreliable government network environments. Standard web scraping of Agmarknet ASPX pages using browser automation frameworks (e.g., Selenium) proved brittle, as dynamic pagination required arbitrary sleep statements and headless driver deployments in Linux environments triggered synchronization errors. 

We replaced web scraping with REST API ingestion from the Open Government Data (OGD) platform, using the \texttt{httpx} library for asynchronous requests and setting strict timeout limits ($\tau_{\text{conn}} = 10\text{s}$, $\tau_{\text{read}} = 45\text{s}$). If the API times out, the provider falls back to cached data in the SQLite database. 

The database operates in Write-Ahead Logging (WAL) mode to support safe concurrent reads and writes. To prevent data corruption from duplicate runs, we implement database-level deduplication via a composite unique index:
\begin{equation}
\text{Index} = (\text{commodity\_slug}, \text{market\_date}, \text{state}, \text{market\_name}, \text{variety})
\end{equation}

All incoming dates are normalized using a multi-format parsing engine, mapping strings like `05/06/2026' or `2026/06/05' to the standard ISO format (`2026-06-05`).

\subsection{Analytics Engine}
LLMs struggle with basic arithmetic calculations, often generating inaccurate averages, percentages, and spreads when prompted with raw tables. To mitigate this, we pre-compute all calculations using the Python \texttt{pandas} library. The engine outputs a structured ``Fact Package'' containing:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Volume-Weighted Average Price}:
    \begin{equation}
    P_{\text{vwa}} = \frac{\sum (P_{\text{modal}, i} \cdot V_i)}{\sum V_i}
    \end{equation}
    where $P_{\text{modal}, i}$ is the modal price and $V_i$ is the arrival volume of market $i$.
    \item \textbf{MSP Deviation}:
    \begin{equation}
    \Delta_{\text{msp}} = \frac{P_{\text{vwa}} - P_{\text{msp}}}{P_{\text{msp}}} \times 100\%
    \end{equation}
    \item \textbf{Historical Price Delta}:
    \begin{equation}
    \Delta_{\text{hist}} = P_{\text{vwa}, t} - P_{\text{vwa}, t-1}
    \end{equation}
\end{enumerate}

By injecting pre-calculated floats directly into the prompt context, we eliminate mathematical reasoning errors in the LLM.

\subsection{Content and Prompts Layer}
The generation phase uses Google Gemini. Prompt engineering is governed by strict rules in \texttt{system\_prompt.txt}. System-level constraints restrict the model's vocabulary to the pre-computed Fact Package. All speculative adjectives (e.g., ``skyrocketing rates''), market sentiment guesses (e.g., ``buyers are cautious''), and advice to farmers (e.g., ``hold your crop'') are strictly prohibited. The output is requested in structured JSON matching the \texttt{ArticleOutput} schema.

\subsection{Trust and Validation Layer}
The Trust Layer is the primary safeguard against hallucinated text. It executes three validation stages:

\subsubsection{Claim Support Validation}
The raw body text is parsed into paragraph blocks. The system checks each paragraph for ungrounded speculative terms, including:
\begin{equation*}
\text{Keywords} \in \{\text{demand}, \text{supply}, \text{sentiment}, \text{outlook}, \text{future}, \text{expect}\}
\end{equation*}
If any paragraph contains these forbidden keywords (and they are not explicitly justified by the seasonal calendar notes in the fact package), the entire paragraph is stripped from the body, and the \texttt{unsupported\_claims\_count} is incremented. The percentage of supported claims ($P_{\text{supported}}$) is computed as:
\begin{equation}
P_{\text{supported}} = \frac{N_{\text{total\_paragraphs}} - N_{\text{unsupported}}}{N_{\text{total\_paragraphs}}} \times 100\%
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Dynamic Report Classification}
The report is classified based on available evidence, preventing the LLM from making sweeping claims on scarce data:
\begin{equation}
\text{Report Type} = 
\begin{cases} 
\text{TREND\_REPORT} & \text{if } t-1 \text{ data exists} \\
\text{MARKET\_REPORT} & \text{if } N_{\text{markets}} > 1 \text{ (no trend)} \\
\text{PRICE\_SNAPSHOT} & \text{if } N_{\text{markets}} = 1 \text{ (no trend)}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Credibility and Confidence Scoring}
The system calculates a credibility score ($C$) out of 1.0 (equivalent to $100\%$) based on data source reliability and claim quality:
\begin{equation}
C = C_{\text{base}} \cdot \gamma_{\text{records}} - \sum \text{Penalties}
\end{equation}
where $C_{\text{base}}$ is defined by the source:
\begin{equation}
C_{\text{base}} = 
\begin{cases} 
0.85 & \text{if } \text{LIVE} \\
0.80 & \text{if } \text{CACHE} \\
0.60 & \text{if } \text{MOCK} 
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
The volume scaling factor $\gamma_{\text{records}}$ rewards datasets with more records:
\begin{equation}
\gamma_{\text{records}} = \min\left(1.0, \frac{N_{\text{records}}}{30}\right)
\end{equation}
Penalties are subtracted for unsupported claims ($\delta_{\text{unsupported}} = 0.05 \text{ per claim}$) and local fallback usage ($\delta_{\text{fallback}} = 0.15$). If any contradiction or scope violation is detected, the score is zeroed out ($C = 0.0$).

\subsubsection{Publishing Gate}
The article's final status is determined by the credibility score ($C$):
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Published ($C \ge 0.75$):} Deployed automatically. Requires $P_{\text{supported}} \ge 95\%$, $0$ contradictions, and $0$ scope violations.
    \item \textbf{Review Required ($0.40 \le C < 0.75$):} Saved for manual approval.
    \item \textbf{Blocked ($C < 0.40$):} Reverted; never shown to users.
\end{itemize}


% ==================== ALGORITHMS AND FORMAL LOGIC ====================
\section{Algorithms and Formal Logic}

This section describes the core logic of the MandiBhav pipeline, formalized as pseudocode procedures.

\subsection{Data Ingestion, Caching, and Fallback Strategy}
Algorithm~\ref{alg:ingest} outlines the process of retrieving, validating, and storing Agmarknet mandi data for a target date.

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Robust Data Ingestion with Local Cache Fallback}, label={alg:ingest}]
Procedure IngestMandiData(target_date, use_demo_mode):
    1.  normalized_date <- ParseAndNormalizeDate(target_date)
    2.  If DBContainsRecordsForDate(normalized_date) Then:
    3.      status <- "CACHE"
    4.      Return GetRecordsFromDB(normalized_date), status
    5.  EndIf
    6.  
    7.  If use_demo_mode Then:
    8.      url <- BuildOGDUrl(commodity="Soyabean", state="Maharashtra", market="Nagpur APMC")
    9.  Else:
    10.     url <- BuildOGDUrl(commodity="Soyabean")
    11. EndIf
    12. 
    13. For attempt <- 1 To MAX_RETRIES Do:
    14.     Try:
    15.         raw_json <- FetchHTTP(url, timeout_conn=10s, timeout_read=45s)
    16.         records <- CleanAndValidateJSON(raw_json)
    17.         If Length(records) > 0 Then:
    18.             SaveToDB(records, deduplicate=True)
    19.             status <- "LIVE"
    20.             Return records, status
    21.         EndIf
    22.     Catch TimeoutError, ConnectionError:
    23.         Wait(2^attempt * 1s)
    24.     EndTry
    25. EndFor
    26. 
    27. // API Failed: Activate Fallback Strategy
    28. historical_records <- QueryLatestHistoricalDB(normalized_date)
    29. If Length(historical_records) > 0 Then:
    30.     status <- "MOCK"
    31.     Return historical_records, status
    32. Else:
    33.     Raise IngestionError("No live, cached, or historical data available.")
    34. EndIf
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Claim Validation and Speculation Stripping}
Algorithm~\ref{alg:validation} shows how the Trust Layer checks LLM-generated reports for speculation and prunes unsupported paragraphs.

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Post-Generation Speculation Stripping}, label={alg:validation}]
Procedure ValidateClaims(article_body, fact_package):
    1.  paragraphs <- SplitIntoParagraphs(article_body)
    2.  supported_paragraphs <- []
    3.  unsupported_count <- 0
    4.  forbidden_words <- ["demand", "supply", "sentiment", "outlook", "future", "expect"]
    5.  
    6.  For Each para In paragraphs Do:
    7.      contains_forbidden <- False
    8.      For Each word In forbidden_words Do:
    9.          If CaseInsensitiveContains(para, word) Then:
    10.             contains_forbidden <- True
    11.             Break
    12.         EndIf
    13.     EndFor
    14.     
    15.     // Check if the forbidden term is justified by fact package
    16.     If contains_forbidden Then:
    17.         If IsFactJustified(para, fact_package) Then:
    18.             supported_paragraphs.Append(para)
    19.         Else:
    20.             unsupported_count <- unsupported_count + 1
    21.         EndIf
    22.     Else:
    23.         supported_paragraphs.Append(para)
    24.     EndIf
    25. EndFor
    26. 
    27. cleaned_body <- Join(supported_paragraphs, separator="\n\n")
    28. p_supported <- (Length(supported_paragraphs) / Length(paragraphs)) * 100.0
    29. Return cleaned_body, unsupported_count, p_supported
\end{lstlisting}

\subsection{Credibility Scoring and Publishing Decision}
Algorithm~\ref{alg:scoring} determines the final credibility score and publishing decision for a generated report.

\begin{lstlisting}[caption={Credibility Scorer and Publishing Gate}, label={alg:scoring}]
Procedure CalculateCredibilityAndGate(records, source_status, unsupported_count, p_supported, contradictions, scope_violations):
    1.  If source_status == "LIVE" Then:
    2.      c_base <- 0.85
    3.  ElseIf source_status == "CACHE" Then:
    4.      c_base <- 0.80
    5.  Else:
    6.      c_base <- 0.60
    7.  EndIf
    8.  
    9.  n_records <- Length(records)
    10. gamma_records <- Min(1.0, n_records / 30.0)
    11. 
    12. penalties <- unsupported_count * 0.05
    13. If source_status == "MOCK" Then:
    14.     penalties <- penalties + 0.15
    15. EndIf
    16. 
    17. credibility_score <- (c_base * gamma_records) - penalties
    18. credibility_score <- Max(0.0, credibility_score)
    19. 
    20. // Security Interlocks
    21. If contradictions > 0 Or scope_violations > 0 Then:
    22.     credibility_score <- 0.0
    23. EndIf
    24. 
    25. // Publishing Decision
    26. If credibility_score >= 0.75 And p_supported >= 95.0 Then:
    27.     decision <- "PUBLISHED"
    28. ElseIf credibility_score >= 0.40 Then:
    29.     decision <- "REVIEW_REQUIRED"
    30. Else:
    31.     decision <- "BLOCKED"
    32. EndIf
    33. 
    34. Return credibility_score, decision
\end{lstlisting}


% ==================== SYSTEM EVALUATION ====================
\section{System Evaluation and Empirical Validation}

A validation run was conducted by clearing all records from the SQLite database for the target date `2026-06-05`, then executing:
\begin{verbatim}
python main.py --mode demo --date 2026-06-05 --publish
\end{verbatim}

The run completed in 43 seconds. During execution, the OGD API returned a success payload containing Nagpur market data. However, the Gemini API returned a $429$ Rate Limit Error during generation. The pipeline handled this by activating the \textbf{Local Fallback Engine}, generating a structured report from local templates and the pre-computed Fact Package.

The validation run results are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:run_results}.

